  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a simple concept for designing a bioresponsive nanomaterial. On the left, a flask symbolizes the chemical design stage, where three key properties are combined: the material is shown as biocompatible, reactive, and able to pro-assemble. This suggests the nanomaterial is intentionally engineered so it is safe for biological settings while also primed to undergo controlled structural changes.\n\nOn the right, the designed material is placed in a cell-like environment containing recognizable organelles. A small blue particle enters the cell and is exposed to intracellular stimuli. In response, it changes and then undergoes self-assembly, illustrated by separate green puzzle-like pieces coming together into a larger connected structure. The arrows indicate that the trigger from the cellular environment drives this assembly process.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a design-to-function pathway: chemists create a material with suitable biological compatibility and reactivity, and once inside cells, local stimuli activate it to assemble into a larger nanostructure. The central idea is that molecular design enables environment-responsive assembly specifically within a biological context.",
